  "articleBody": "Getting ready for new kitchen cabinets is exciting, but a little prep work goes a long way to ensure a smooth installation. First off, make sure your space is 'dry-in' – that means framing, electrical, plumbing rough-ins, drywall, and most of your flooring should be done. This keeps moisture at bay and protects your new cabinets from damage. Next, you’ll want to inspect those new cabinets for any damage and double-check them against your layout plans. Trust me, it's easier to catch issues before they're installed. You'll also need to identify the high spots in your floor and walls, then establish level reference lines for both your base and wall cabinets. This is crucial for a perfectly level final product. Don't forget to mark your stud locations; those wall cabinets need to be securely fastened! Finally, make sure you know the typical clearances, like the 18 inches generally recommended between your countertop and the bottom of your wall cabinets. Having everything ready to go makes a huge difference. If you're feeling overwhelmed, give HH Remodeling LLC a call. We can help with everything from planning to the final touches.",
